Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement. Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect carveouts? Option A: No Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement: “Material Adverse Effect” means any change, event, or condition that, individually or in aggregate with other effects, has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on the business, financial condition, or operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries. Notwithstanding the foregoing, for purposes of determining whether a Material Adverse Effect has occurred, the following shall be excluded: (i) any delay in closing or consummation of the transaction arising solely from general economic slowdowns, fluctuations in financial markets, or statutory bank holidays; (ii) any postponement of closing due solely to regulatory halts imposed by a governmental authority, provided such halts do not extend beyond a period of ten (10) business days, in which event the delay shall not be considered a Material Adverse Effect; (iii) any force majeure events, including natural disasters, that directly affect operations, provided that such events are promptly communicated by the Company. However, any delay or failure that arises from issues other than those specifically excluded above, including technical, operational, or internal governance issues impacting the ability to consummate the transaction, shall be deemed to trigger a Material Adverse Effect. This Agreement expressly states that while certain external and regulatory delays are carved out for purposes of the MAE determination, a delay attributable to other factors impacting the 'ability to consummate' shall be fully within the scope of a Material Adverse Effect.
