Instruction: Read the following excerpt from a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by selecting the option that best characterizes the agreement.

Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement Excerpt:
“Material Adverse Effect” means any event, change, occurrence or circumstance that either (i) has, or would reasonably be expected to have, a material adverse effect on the business, assets, financial condition or operations of the Company, or (ii) would, or would reasonably be expected to, impair or delay the Company’s ability to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement. 

Provided, however, that with respect to the ability to consummate the transaction, no event or change shall be deemed to constitute a Material Adverse Effect if it is solely attributable to any of the following carveouts:
(a) general economic downturns or adverse market conditions affecting industries broadly;
(b) short-term fluctuations in the trading price of the Company’s common stock;
(c) natural disasters or other force majeure events, including hurricanes, earthquakes, or pandemics;
(d) any adverse impact resulting exclusively from the public announcement or pendency of this Agreement.

Notwithstanding the foregoing, if any event—even one falling within these exceptions—demonstrates a disproportionate adverse impact on the consummation process relative to that experienced by similar companies in the industry, then the incremental impact may be considered when determining if a Material Adverse Effect has occurred.

